<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Sheikhdom of Mohammerah</h1><p class="st-meta">1812 CE – 1925 CE (approx.) · capital Mohammerah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Arab sheikhdom at the mouth of the Karun whose last ruler, Sheikh Khaz'al, protected Britain's first great oil refinery at Abadan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Muhaysin, a branch of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banu-kab-sheikhdom">Banu Ka'b sheikhdom</a>, developed the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Mohammerah</a> (modern Khorramshahr) at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karun-river">Karun</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Shatt al-Arab</a> in the early 19th century. Under Hajji Jabir Khan it became the dominant power in the Arab lowlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, known as Arabistan, nominally under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sheikhs profited from river and Gulf trade, especially after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karun-river">Karun</a> was opened to foreign steamers in 1888. Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazal-ibn-jabir">Khaz'al ibn Jabir</a>, who took power in 1897 by killing his brother Mizal, became a semi-independent prince. In 1909 he leased land on Abadan island to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bp">Anglo-Persian Oil Company</a> for its refinery, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> gave him guarantees of protection. He ruled from his palace at Failiyeh near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Mohammerah</a>, dealing directly with British officials.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazal-ibn-jabir">Khaz'al</a> rebelled against the central government in 1924, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan's</a> army entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, and in 1925 he was arrested and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, where he died in 1936. The sheikhdom was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pahlavi-iran">Pahlavi Iran</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sheikhdom-of-mohammerah.jpg" alt="Sheikhdom of Mohammerah" width="320" height="374"><figcaption>Image: East India Company, the Board of Control, the India Office, or other British Government Department · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tribal_and_Communications_Map_of_Area_No_13_(%E2%80%98Arabistan).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1812 CE – 1925 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mohammerah</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jabir Khan, Mizal Khan, Khaz'al ibn Jabir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Mohammerah, Arabistan, Muhammara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=sheikhdom-of-mohammerah" aria-label="Sheikhdom of Mohammerah on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6081 1024 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sheikhdom of Mohammerah lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M4997 651h0M6298 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6401 1280h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6401 1280h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iran. <a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=sheikhdom-of-mohammerah">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
